

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Two more questions, then I'll lay out the design. This one's about how a shared module actually gets wired in, because
  the repo currently has two incompatible worlds.

  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a plain browser script loaded by [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m ([38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m) with no module system at all, while
  [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are CommonJS using [38;5;153mrequire[39m. There's no bundler, no build step, and no dependencies in
  [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m. A module that "works across the app" has to pick one of these worlds.

  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153midentity.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m and imports it. Native, no dependencies,
    and future forms get a clean named import. One real catch: module scripts won't load over [38;5;153mfile://[39m, so you'd need to
    open the app through a local HTTP server instead of double-clicking [38;5;153mindex.html[39m.
  - [1mGlobal script[0m — a second [38;5;153m<script src="identity.js">[39m before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, exposing something like [38;5;153mwindow.Identity[39m. Zero
    friction, still works from [38;5;153mfile://[39m, matches the current code exactly. But it's a global, load order becomes
    load-bearing, and there's no import to make the dependency visible.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — solves interop with the CommonJS [38;5;153msrc/[39m files properly, at the cost of a build step and a dependency
    tree in a repo that currently has neither. I'd call this YAGNI unless a bundler is coming anyway.

  My recommendation is ES modules: the whole point of this change is reuse by code that doesn't exist yet, and an
  explicit [38;5;153mimport[39m is what makes that reuse legible. The [38;5;153mfile://[39m limitation is the one thing that might rule it out, so
  tell me if opening [38;5;153mindex.html[39m directly matters to your workflow.
